      "outputType": "markdown",
      "output": "## Synthesized sound function: `sfx(name)`\n\nThis code synthesizes every sound with the Web Audio API and loads no audio files. It is 13 lines long and runs nothing at the top level. The AudioContext is created only inside `sfxInit()`, which you call from a user-input handler. Any `sfx()` call made before that is silently ignored.\n\n```javascript\nlet AC = null;\nfunction sfxInit(){ if(!AC){ const C = window.AudioContext || window.webkitAudioContext; if(!C) return; AC = new C(); } if(AC.state === 'suspended') AC.resume(); }\nfunction tone(f1, f2, dur, type, vol, delay){ const t = AC.currentTime + (delay || 0), o = AC.createOscillator(), g = AC.createGain(); o.type = type; o.frequency.setValueAtTime(f1, t); o.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(f2, t + dur); g.gain.setValueAtTime(0.0001, t); g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(vol, t + 0.01); g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.0001, t + dur); o.connect(g).connect(AC.destination); o.start(t); o.stop(t + dur + 0.02); }\nfunction noise(dur, f1, f2, vol){ const t = AC.currentTime, n = Math.floor(AC.sampleRate * dur), b = AC.createBuffer(1, n, AC.sampleRate), d = b.getChannelData(0); for(let i = 0; i < n; i++) d[i] = Math.random() * 2 - 1; const s = AC.createBufferSource(), f = AC.createBiquadFilter(), g = AC.createGain(); s.buffer = b; f.type = 'lowpass'; f.frequency.setValueAtTime(f1, t); f.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(f2, t + dur); g.gain.setValueAtTime(vol, t); g.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.0001, t + dur); s.connect(f).connect(g).connect(AC.destination); s.start(t); }\nfunction sfx(name){ if(!AC || AC.state === 'closed') return;\n  switch(name){\n    case 'fish':    tone(520, 1040, 0.12, 'square', 0.15); break;\n    case 'gold':    [523, 659, 784, 1047].forEach((f, i) => tone(f, f * 1.01, 0.4, 'triangle', 0.12, i * 0.04)); break;\n    case 'bump':    tone(140, 50, 0.18, 'sine', 0.5); noise(0.08, 800, 100, 0.2); break;\n    case 'splash':  noise(0.6, 3000, 300, 0.4); tone(400, 120, 0.25, 'sine', 0.1); break;\n    case 'count':   tone(660, 660, 0.15, 'square', 0.12); break;\n    case 'go':      tone(880, 880, 0.4, 'square', 0.14); break;\n    case 'whistle': for(let i = 0; i < 2; i++) tone(2000, 2300, 0.35, 'sine', 0.2, i * 0.45); break; } }\n```\n\n### Sound names\n| Name | Sound | Game event |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fish` | Short rising square-wave blip (520→1040 Hz) | Penguin collects a normal fish (+1) |\n| `gold` | Bright arpeggiated C-major chord (C5-E5-G5-C6), triangle wave | Penguin collects a golden fish (+3) |\n| `bump` | Low sine thud (140→50 Hz) plus a short muffled noise tap | Two penguins collide and bounce apart |\n| `splash` | Noise with a falling low-pass filter (3000→300 Hz) plus a falling \"plop\" tone | Penguin's centre leaves the floe and it falls in |\n| `count` | Short 660 Hz beep | Each of the countdown steps 3, 2, 1 |\n| `go` | Longer, higher 880 Hz beep | \"GO!\" at the end of the countdown |\n| `whistle` | Two high sine whistle blasts (2000→2300 Hz) | Timer reaches 0, before the results panel |\n\n### How to hook it into the game\n- **Unlock audio first.** Call `sfxInit()` as the first statement inside your existing `keydown` handler, for example `window.addEventListener('keydown', e => { sfxInit(); /* game input */ })`.\n  - The menu needs **2/3/4** or **Enter** to start, so the context always exists before the countdown.\n  - `sfxInit()` is safe to call on every key press. It creates the context once and resumes it if the browser suspended it.\n- **Call the sounds from game logic:**\n  - countdown tick → `sfx('count')`, then `sfx('go')`\n  - fish pickup → `sfx(f.golden ? 'gold' : 'fish')`\n  - collision response → `sfx('bump')`\n  - fall detection → `sfx('splash')`\n  - round end → `sfx('whistle')`\n- **Throttle bumps.** Two penguins can stay in contact for several frames, so add a short per-pair cooldown (for example 150 ms) before calling `sfx('bump')`.\n\n### Unverified\nThis code has not been run in a browser. These points are untested:\n- that the code runs without errors,\n- the actual timbre and loudness of each sound,\n- the volume balance between the sounds,\n- that the context is unlocked correctly in Chrome, Firefox and Safari (including `webkitAudioContext`),\n- behaviour when many sounds overlap quickly, such as frequent bumps and fish pickups at once."
